WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.320
I was born in 1925.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.880
I grew up not long after
the end of the First World War,

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.760
and then I lived through the next war.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.720
My grandfather was killed
at the beginning of World War I.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.920
He led a charge
and didn't survive it.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.520
At home we had a painting
of my grandfather

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.360
and for years I ate all my meals
in front of that painting,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.760
which was very frightening
for a six-year-old child.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.600
My father was in the army,
and on 28 May 1940

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.840
he was captured by the Germans.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.640
He was a prisoner of war in Germany
for five years.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.840
I missed him a lot...

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.960
I was at an age when boys
really need their father.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.320
We had gone to stay with friends.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.840
Here are my mother, my sisters and I
sitting in a trench at their place.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.240
In 1944, I was able to join
the Belgian army

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.520
because the Piron Brigade was recruiting.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.560
My friends and I did our training
with the British army

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.440
and I became a radio operator.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.440
We went to the front in Holland.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.160
We stayed there until the war ended
on 8 May 1945.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.720
There's no more war now.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.840
We've had 75 years of peace and fortunately
we now have the European Union.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.480
Thanks to that,
people have got used...

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.480
to talking
instead of fighting.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.560
Europe unites us.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.800
Europe has a civilising effect.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.560
I've met German people
in my professional life.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.800
I've never had a problem with that.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.520
Our children, our grandchildren
and our great-grandchildren

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.040
have never experienced war.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.560
In historical terms,
that's exceptional.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.080
They have no idea how lucky they are.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.920
We've come to Hotton,
to the British and Commonwealth cemetery.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.160
It's in the Ardennes, in Belgium.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.280
That's why I wanted to bring
my grandson here,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.680
for him to know how,
75 years ago, boys his age

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.640
fought here in Belgium
and in the surrounding area

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.600
so that we might be here today.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.168
People tend to forget.
There's talk of borders again.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.960
We're seeing a return to futile things
and aggressiveness.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.640
I've always marvelled at the fact that,
from such faraway countries,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.720
boys aged 19 or 20 came here

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.520
and died here...

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.160
I find it incredible.

